      "text": "## 4. Adding audio and video\n\nHTML provides `<audio>` and `<video>` elements for media playback. The `controls` attribute asks the browser to display playback controls such as play, pause, volume, or seeking. Without controls, visitors may have no obvious way to start or manage playback unless you build another accessible control system.\n\nUse the `src` attribute to point to a media file, or place one or more `<source>` elements inside the media element. Multiple sources can let browsers choose a format they support. Each `<source>` commonly uses `src` for the file and `type` for its media type.\n\nFor video, consider adding `width` or responsive CSS and a `poster` image if you want a preview before playback. Captions are important for people who are deaf or hard of hearing, and they also help visitors who cannot play sound. A `<track>` element can reference a captions file, often in WebVTT format, with `kind=\"captions\"`, a `src`, and a `label`.\n\nUse `autoplay` carefully. Browsers often restrict autoplay, especially when sound is enabled, and unexpected playback can disturb visitors. Provide controls and respect accessibility and user choice."
